## Introduction
Since the discovery and characterization of hemoglobin in the 1800s, significant effort has been focused on designing synthetic transition metal complexes that can bind $0_{2}$ for a diverse range of applications, including oxidation catalysis, electrocatalytic oxygen reduction, and the separation of oxygen from air.1–4 Over the last several decades, metal−dinitrogen complexes have also been actively investigated, with much attention focused on catalytic nitrogen activation, the separation of nitrogen from methane-rich natural gas, and the purification of ${\sf N}_{2}$ from air.3,5–7 For each of these purposes, the binding strength of $0_{2}$ and/or ${\sf N}_{2}$ has a significant influence on the performance of the investigated material. This is especially true for the design of an adsorbent that can separate $0_{2}$ from ${\bf N}_{2}$ as the material must be able to selectively bind one molecule over the other at ambient temperatures and to do so reversibly.2 An adsorbent that could perform this separation at moderate conditions with high selectivity would be desirable for industrial and medical processes that require purified $^{0_{2},8}$ as the conventional method to separate $0_{2}$ from air at a large scale involves cryogenic distillation, an energy-intensive and expensive process.9 While cation-exchanged zeolites are currently used as adsorbents for air separation purposes,8 they typically favor the adsorption of ${\sf N}_{2}$ over $0_{2}$ and are inefficient compared to $0_{2}$ -selective materials for the production of $0_{2}$ via air separation.
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) have been widely studied for applications involving the binding of small molecules due to their well-defined structures and high degree of tunability made possible by modifying the inorganic nodes and organic linkers that compose each material.10–15 MOFs with coordinatively unsaturated metal sites, also known as open metal sites, are of particular interest for the selective binding of $0_{2}$ or ${\sf N}_{2}$ due to the possibility of charge transfer between the adsorbate and exposed metal cations.16
Despite active research in this area,17–24 significant improvements are still needed to achieve a greater $0_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ selectivity at near-ambient conditions before MOFs with open metal sites can be used for the purification of $0_{2}$ from air. Several MOFs with $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{II})$ open metal sites, including $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ $\mathrm{\Delta[H_{3}B T C}$ $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid) and Cr-BTT $(\mathrm{Cr}_{3}[(\mathrm{Cr}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{BTT})_{8}]_{2},$ $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTT}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ benzene-1,3,5-tris( $1H$ -tetrazole)),20,21 show strong and selective binding of $0_{2}$ over ${\sf N}_{2}$ but exhibit a loss in capacity with multiple cycles. Oftentimes, MOFs with open metal sites exhibit only weak physisorption of $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2},$ especially at near-ambient temperatures.23,25 On both ends of this spectrum, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{D}0\mathrm{B}\mathrm{D}\mathsf{C})$ $(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{D}0\mathrm{B}\mathrm{D}0=2,5$ -dihydroxybenzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid) reversibly binds $0_{2}$ over ${\sf N}_{2}$ at low temperatures, but temperatures above $226\mathrm{~K~}$ result in the formation of superoxo species that irreversibly oxidize the framework.19 Among the more promising MOFs investigated for $0_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ separation, Co-BTTri $\mathrm{(Co_{3}[(C o_{4}C l)_{3}(B T T r i)_{8}]_{2},}$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTTri}}&{{}=}&{1,3,5\mathrm{-tri}(1H\mathrm{-}1,2,3\mathrm{-t}}\end{array}$ riazol-5-yl)benzene) and CoBDTriP $\mathrm{(Co_{3}[(C o_{4}C l)_{3}(B D T r i P)_{8}]_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{H_{3}B D T r i P}=5{,}5^{\prime}{\mathrm{.}}$ -(5-( $1H\cdot$ -pyrazol-4-yl)-1,3-phenylene)bis(1H-1,2,3-triazole)) can reversibly bind $0_{2}$ over ${\sf N}_{2},$ , although their selectivity towards $0_{2}$ is significantly reduced at temperatures above $195\mathrm{K}.^{22}$
Given the enormous library of inorganic nodes and organic linkers26,27 and the significant time involved in synthesizing, characterizing, and testing new MOFs for $0_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ separation, there is a need for design rules that can be used to predictively tune the $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ affinities at the open metal sites of MOFs. Computational screening methods based on first-principles calculations are appealing avenues to achieve this goal, as multiple distinct MOF families can be studied in a systematic fashion and on a much larger number of materials than would be feasible experimentally. However, much of the prior computational work has been carried out on a small scale, often focusing on MOFs directly motivated by experiments $28\mathrm{-}30$ or on the prototypical (yet structurally unrelated) $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathbf{D}0\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C})$ and $\mathrm{{M_{3}}(B T C)_{2}}$ MOF families.25,28,29,31–33 This has limited the identification of broadly generalizable principles that can be used to identify and predictively design new MOFs for applications involving the adsorption of $0_{2}$ or ${\sf N}_{2}$ at redox-active open metal sites. Nonetheless, for these systems, theory-driven studies have proven quite useful for understanding the effect of metal identity,32 preferred gas binding geometries,29,32 electronic structure of the $[\mathsf{M}-\mathsf{O}_{2}]^{2+}$ species,28 and dynamics of competitive gas adsorption.31 Prior work has also shown that the $0_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ selectivity can be correlated with the difference in computed binding energies,32 although the ambient temperature $0_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ selectivity is often significantly lower than that at cryogenic conditions.22,23 The discovery of MOFs that selectively bind $0_{2}$ over ${\sf N}_{2}$ at room temperature remains a significant challenge.
In the present study, we leverage a high-throughput periodic density functional theory (DFT) workflow34 to investigate $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption at the open metal sites of several MOF families (Figure 1). We have specifically chosen structurally related pairs of MOFs to aid in identifying structure−property relationships that can be used to guide the design of MOFs with enhanced affinities for $0_{2}$ and/or ${\sf N}_{2}$ at open metal sites, primarily (albeit not exclusively) for air separation applications. These MOF families include $\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{II})_{2}(\mathrm{DOBDC}),^{35-3}$ 7 $\mathsf{M}(\mathrm{II})_{2}(\mathsf{D S B D C})$ $(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DSBDC}~=~2,5\$ -disulfhydrylbenzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid),38,39 $\mathrm{M(II)_{2}C l_{2}(B B T A)}$ $\begin{array}{l l l}{\left({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BBTA}}\ =\ }&{1H,5H.\right.}\end{array}$ benzo(1,2-d:4,5-d′)bistriazole),40–42 $\mathrm{M(II)}_{2}\mathrm{(OH)}_{2}\mathrm{(BBTA)},43\mathrm{-}45$ $\mathrm{M(II)M(III)}_{2}(\upmu_{3}{\cdot}0)(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ $\mathrm{\:\cdot\mathrm{H_{2}B D C=}}$ benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid),46,47 and $\mathrm{M(III)_{3}(\upmu_{3}-0)(B D C)_{3}O H},\mathrm{\}46,47$ , which were selected due to their ability to support several coordinatively unsaturated, first row transition metal cations as well as their structural tunability.
As a result of our computational screening workflow and the design rules established in this work, we predict that exchanging the $\scriptstyle|\mathsf{A-C l^{-}}$ ligands of $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ with $\scriptstyle\left|\mu-0\mathrm{H}\right|^{.}$ ligands to form the isostructural framework $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ should significantly enhance the $0_{2}$ affinity at the open Co(II) sites without strengthening their interaction with ${\sf N}_{2}$ . This prediction is further validated experimentally, providing support that MOFs with open metal sites that are otherwise redox-inactive can be made to chemisorb $0_{2}$ by means of post-synthetic ligand-exchange, particularly when using basic bridging ligands that also enable H-bonding interactions with the reduced $0_{2}$ adsorbate.
Methods
## Constructing Initial Model Structures
To aid in the identification of structure−property relationships, the members of each MOF family were modeled as being in an isostructural series using the coordination geometries shown in Figure 1. For brevity, we will often refer to these MOFs as MOF-74 or MOF-74-S, MAF-X (MAF $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ Metal−Azolate Framework, $\mathrm{X=}$ bridging anion), and MIL-88B $\mathrm{\bf~MIL}=$ Materials Institut Lavoisier) or MIL-88B-OH, as shown in Figure 1. We considered the presence of $\mathsf{V}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cr^{2+}}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{N i^{2+}}.$ , $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ cations for MOFs with $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ binding sites and $S c^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Ti^{3+}}$ , $\mathsf{V}^{3+}$ $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}.$ , $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn^{3+}}}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}.$ , $\mathsf{C o}^{3+},$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ cations for MOFs with $\mathsf{M}^{3+}$ binding sites. The MOF-74, MOF-74-S, and MAF-X families support divalent metal cations, whereas the MIL-88B family can support formally trivalent (in the case of MIL-88B-OH) or mixed divalent/trivalent (in the case of MIL-88B) cations depending on the presence of terminal OH groups, as depicted in Figure S1.
Some of the metal cations considered in this work have yet to be experimentally incorporated in the investigated MOF families; nonetheless, a wide range of $3d$ metals were considered for the purposes of identifying trends related to $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption at open metal sites in MOFs. As adopted in prior computational studies of MOFs,48 only a single $3d$ metal cation was included per primitive simulation unit cell during the screening process to reduce the computational expense and prevent challenges associated with accurately identifying complex ground state spin orderings. The remaining metal species were exchanged with closed-shell cations, namely $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ or $\mathbb{A}\mathbb{l}^{3+}$ depending on the formal oxidation state of the metal in the assynthesized state (Figure S1). Similar structures have been achieved experimentally for a variety of MOFs, such as Mg1.9Fe0.1(DOBDC)49 and Fe-doped Al-MIL-53.50 Various mixed-metal motifs have also been incorporated in the trimetallic nodes of the PCN-250 $\mathrm{\mathop{PCN}=}$ Porous Coordination Network) and MIL-88 families.47,51
Adsorbates were systematically added to the open metal sites in an automated fashion using the MOF Adsorbate Initializer (MAI) program. $34,52\ O_{2}$ conventionally adsorbs to mononuclear transition metal complexes in either a bent, end-on $(\eta^{1})$ mode or a side-on $(\eta^{2})$ mode, whereas stable $\mathsf{M}-\mathsf{N}_{2}$ complexes are generally only found with near-linear, end-on $(\eta^{1})$ adsorption modes.3,53 The initialized structures generated using MAI were constructed accordingly. For $\begin{array}{r}{0_{2},}\end{array}$ an $\eta^{1}$ mode with an $\mathbf{M}{-}0{-}0$ bond angle of $120^{\circ}$ and an $\eta^{2}$ mode with an $\ensuremath{\mathsf{M}}-\ensuremath{\mathsf{O}}_{\ensuremath{\mathrm{MP}}-0}$ bond angle of $90^{\circ}$ were constructed, where $\mathsf{M}$ is the metal binding site and $0_{\mathrm{MP}}$ represents the midpoint between the two O atoms in molecular oxygen. For ${\bf N}_{2}$ , an $\eta^{1}$ mode with an $\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{N}$ bond angle of $180^{\circ}$ was constructed. In all cases, a single guest molecule was introduced to the Niggli-reduced unit cell, and the lowest energy conformations resulting from the DFT optimization of these initial structures were considered for further analysis. The CrystalNN bonding topology algorithm54 implemented in Pymatgen55 was used to categorize if the lowest energy adsorbate configuration exhibits an $\eta^{1}$ or $\eta^{2}$ adsorption mode (or approximately no binding at all, which we denote $\eta^{0}.$ ) following structural optimization.
## Quantum-Chemical Calculations
For the DFT screening procedure, adsorption energies were evaluated using the Vienna ab initio Simulation Package (VASP) v.5.4.156,57 with v.5.4 of the VASP-recommended58 projectoraugmented wave (PAW) pseudopotentials.59 The unit cell shapes and volumes for the diluted MOFs were optimized using the PBE-D3(BJ) level of theory,60–62 as PBE with dispersion corrections has been shown to accurately capture the lattice constants of a variety of MOFs.63,64 Following this, the lattice constants were kept fixed and the atomic positions refined using the $\mathsf{M}06{\cdot}\mathrm{L}$ meta-generalized gradient approximation (GGA) exchange-correlation functional.65 All subsequent calculations involving the adsorption of any guest molecules were carried out at the $\mathtt{M O6-L}$ level of theory with a fixed simulation unit cell. While hybrid-level functionals would be expected to reduce the tendency to overbind $0_{2},^{25}$ their high computational cost precludes their use when screening a large number of MOF−adsorbate systems, each with several possible spin states and adsorption modes. $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption energies computed using GGA and $\mathrm{GGA}{+}U$ approaches will be reported in forthcoming work and show similar structure−property relationships to those reported here.66
As benchmarked and described in detail previously,34 a multi-stage automated workflow built upon the Atomic Simulation Environment67 was used to robustly and efficiently carry out the periodic DFT calculations in this work. The final highaccuracy settings used in the optimization workflow include a $520\mathrm{eV}$ plane-wave kinetic energy cutoff, $k$ -point density of approximately 1000/number of atoms (as computed using Pymatgen’s automatic $k$ -point density generator55), and a force-convergence criterion of $\lvert F_{\mathrm{max}}\rvert<\lvert\bar{0}.0\bar{3}\mathrm{eV}/\bar{\mathrm{A}}.^{34}$ Spin-polarization was considered for each MOF, as summarized in Table S1. The properties of the lowest energy spin state found from this procedure are reported in all analyses. The preconditioned conjugate gradient “all bands simultaneous update of orbitals” algorithm68–70 was used to converge the self-consistent field (SCF), and Gaussian smearing of the band occupancies with a smearing width of $0.01\ \mathrm{eV}$ was employed (with electronic energies extrapolated back to the $0\mathrm{{K}}$ limit). The SCF was considered converged when the change in electronic energy was less than $10^{-6}\mathrm{eV}$ . The accurate-precision keyword was enabled in VASP, and all symmetry operations were disabled. Isolated gasphase species were modeled in the center of a periodic box with $10\textup{\AA}$ of vacuum space on each side of the molecule and $k$ -point sampling at the $\Gamma$ -point only.
$0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption energies were computed as
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\Delta E_{0_{2}}=E_{\mathrm{MOF}-\mathrm{0_{2}}}-E_{\mathrm{MOF}}-E_{\mathrm{0_{2}}}}\\ {\Delta E_{\mathrm{N_{2}}}=E_{\mathrm{MOF}-\mathrm{N_{2}}}-E_{\mathrm{MOF}}-E_{\mathrm{N_{2}}}}\end{array}
$$
where $E_{\mathrm{MOF}-{0_{2}}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{MOF-N_{2}}}$ are the electronic energies of the MOF with an $0_{2}$ or ${\sf N}_{2}$ guest species at the open metal site, $E_{\mathrm{MOF}}$ is the electronic energy of the bare MOF, and $E_{0_{2}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{N}_{2}}$ are the electronic energies of isolated $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2},$ respectively. All computed physicochemical properties (e.g. optimized structures, spin states, binding modes, partial charges, spin densities), and additional methodological details can be found in the Supporting Information.
Periodic DFT calculations of the non-diluted $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ and $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ frameworks were carried out at the PBE-D3(BJ) level of theory with a Hubbard $U$ correction71 of $3.3\mathrm{eV}$ on the $d$ levels of the Co sites chosen because of its ability to reproduce oxidation energies in cobalt oxides.72 This level of theory accurately reproduces both the lattice constants and $0_{2}$ adsorption energy in the structurally similar CoBTTri framework (Table S7). Additional computational details, such as the most stable spin states, can be found in the Supporting Information, with all computational results hosted at the Zenodo repository with DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.2652475.
## Experimental Methods
$\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}(\mathsf{B B T A})$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ were synthesized, characterized, and investigated for their $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake. The MOFs were synthesized based on previously reported procedures,41,44,73 with $\mathrm{Co2(OH)_{2}(B B T A)}$ prepared by soaking $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of the parent $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathbf{BBTA})$ framework overnight in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ of 1 M KOH solution at room temperature. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), ${\sf N}_{2}$ physisorption surface area using the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), and diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS) were carried out to characterize the materials and to confirm the full exchange of $\mathsf{\Pi}\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C l-}$ ligands with $\scriptstyle\mathsf{\mu-O H^{-}}$ in synthesizing $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ . Additional details regarding the experimental methods can be found in the Supporting Information.
## Results
Overview of Screening Results and Periodic Trends
Using a high-throughput periodic DFT workflow,34 we screened the MOFs shown in Figure 1 for the adsorption of $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ . As summarized in Figure 2, a wide spread of $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption energies are observed across the MOF dataset. Nonetheless, the range in $0_{2}$ binding energies is approximately three times larger than that of the ${\sf N}_{2}$ binding energies, suggesting that the chemisorption of $0_{2}$ is more common than that of ${\sf N}_{2}$ at the MOF open metal sites. There are also broad periodic trends in the $0_{2}$ affinity at the open metal sites in MOFs, with early transition metals generally resulting in significantly more exothermic adsorption energies than later transition metals for a given MOF family and oxidation state (Figure 2a). This is consistent with the fact that $d$ orbital energies tend to decrease (i.e. become more stable) with increasing $d$ electron count, which reduces the degree of electron density transferred from the metal to the $0_{2}$ adsorbate.3
Depending on the interaction between the metal center and $0_{2}$ adsorbate, the metal−dioxygen complex can take on one of several possible electronic states that exist on the spectrum of $\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{II})-\mathbf{0}_{2}\leftrightarrow\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{III})-\mathbf{0}_{2^{\circ}}\leftrightarrow\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{IV})-\mathbf{0}_{2}\mathbf{{^{2}}}\cdot$ . By comparing the sum of Bader partial atomic charges on the $0_{2}$ adsorbate with the sum of Bader partial charges on the O atoms of gaseous $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\bf O}_{2}$ ${\mathrm{HO}}_{2},$ and $0_{2},$ it is possible to evaluate the degree of $0_{2}$ reduction for each MOF. As shown in Figure 3a, the exothermicity of $0_{2}$ adsorption is correlated with the degree of charge transfer to the $0_{2}$ adsorbate, implying that redox-active open metal sites are needed for strong $0_{2}$ chemisorption. In addition, the results in Figure 3a indicate that the reduction levels of $0_{2}$ are best thought of as continuous, in contrast with the quantized perspective often invoked in the literature.3
DFT-computed stretching frequencies, bond distances, spin densities, and partial atomic charges of the $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ guest molecules collectively indicate that the MOFs in this work support dioxygen-, superoxo-, and peroxo-like binding of $0_{2}$ whereas the triple bond of ${\sf N}_{2}$ stays largely intact for most of the MOFs (Figure S2). In terms of the most favorable $0_{2}$ binding modes for the MOFs in Figure 1, side-on adsorption was observed at all the $\mathrm{Ti^{3+}}$ $^{3+},\lor^{2+},\lor^{3+},$ , $\mathsf{C r^{3+}}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ sites, whereas a bent, end-on mode occurred for the remaining transition metals (excluding those with negligible binding, such as the $\mathsf{S c}^{3+}$ $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ sites), as shown in Table S1. In most cases, the side-on binding modes are associated with a greater degree of superoxo character than the end-on binding mode (Figure 3a). Select MOFs are shown in Figure 3b to highlight the different $0_{2}$ adsorption behavior across the MOF dataset.
For a given oxidation state, open metal sites composed of early transition metals are more likely to selectively bind $0_{2}$ over ${\sf N}_{2}$ (Figure S5), as has been noted previously.32 Despite this, we caution against the design of MOFs with readily oxidized early transition metals for this application, as the highly exothermic $0_{2}$ adsorption energies are likely to result in irreversible chemisorption or, potentially, decomposition of the framework. This behavior has caused several previously investigated MOFs with early transition metals, such as M-MIL-101 $(\mathsf{M}=\mathrm{Ti},\mathsf{V}),\mathsf{\ensuremath{30}},75$ to be unsuitable for applications where reversible $0_{2}$ adsorption is necessary. Additionally, MOFs with exposed, low-valent early transition metals (particularly $\mathsf{V}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cr^{2+}}]$ have not been widely synthesized to date.20,21,76–78 At the other end of the first row of transition metals, MOFs with Ni, $\mathtt{C u},$ and Zn cations are unlikely to result in high $0_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ selectivities, as they are not predicted to strongly adsorb $0_{2}$ at the metal centers. We instead recommend the identification and design of MOFs with redox-active $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , or ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ sites that can be made to strongly favor the adsorption of $0_{2}$ over ${\sf N}_{2}$ without forming metal−peroxo complexes that are often associated with irreversible $0_{2}$ binding in MOFs.19,75,79
## Trends Between Related MOF Families
By examining the results for structurally related MOF families, it is possible to discern how subtle changes to the linkers and nodes of MOFs can be used to significantly modify the $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ binding strengths. Starting with the MOF-74 and MOF-74-S families, Figure 4 shows that the presence of thiophenoxide groups in MOF-74-S results in weaker adsorption of $0_{2}$ than the phenoxide groups of MOF-74, a trend that holds for all the metal cations considered in this work. The $0_{2}$ adsorption energy trend is the opposite of what might be predicted solely on the basis of electronegativity differences between O and S. Even though the metal centers of MOF-74 have higher partial atomic charges than their corresponding MOF-74-S analogues (Table S2), the former still binds $0_{2}$ more strongly. Although likely not the sole factor, as noted in prior work for a series of ketoiminato/thioiminato Co(II) complexes,80 these trends can be attributed in part to the greater donating character of the oxido (as opposed to sulfido) bridges. Figure 4 also shows that MOF74 tends to bind ${\sf N}_{2}$ more strongly than MOF-74-S. We note that the experimentally synthesized Mn-MOF-74-S structure is known to have seesaw-like open metal sites (Figure S6), in contrast with the M-MOF-74 family and Fe-MOF-74-S.39 This seesaw structure for Mn-MOF-74-S is also predicted to have a lower affinity for $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ than its Mn-MOF-74 counterpart (Table S3).
Significant differences in the $0_{2}$ binding strength are also found between the MAF-Cl and MAF-OH families, which have identical triazolate linkers but different bridging anions connecting the extended chain of metals. Based on the periodic DFT calculations, we predict that the triazolate MOFs with $\upmu\mathrm{.}$ - $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ species generally bind $0_{2}$ more strongly than their analogues with $\mathsf{\Pi}\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C l-}$ species (Figure 5). The only exception is for the Cu- and $\mathrm{Zn}$ -containing MOFs, where changing the bridging ligands does not greatly influence the $0_{2}$ binding affinity since neither $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ nor $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ are expected to be oxidized by $0_{2}$ .
Based on the DFT-optimized structures, we predict that the MAF-OH family allows for H-bonding between the bridging $\scriptstyle\left.\mu-0\mathrm{H}\right.^{\cdot}$ species and the $0_{2}$ species bound to the metal sites (Figure S7), provided the $0_{2}$ molecule has been partially reduced. The presence of H-bonding is believed to stabilize metal−dioxygen species in enzymatic systems, such as hemoglobin,81,82 and can be used to provide further control over the $0_{2}$ adsorption energy in MOFs where this functionality is possible. In addition, the $\scriptstyle|\mathsf{u-C l-}$ ligands and $\scriptstyle\left.\mu-0\mathrm{H}\right.-$ ligands have notably different basicities. Several correlations have been noted in prior work that relate greater basicities of the coordinating ligands to stronger $0_{2}$ affinities,79,83  as is predicted here for MAF-OH. In these cases, the basicity of the ligand acts as a proxy for its donating ability, which serves to stabilize the oxidized state of the metal site. With the exception of proposed $\mathsf{V}^{2+}$ open metal sites as previously discussed, both the MAF-Cl and MAF-OH families are predicted to weakly physisorb ${\sf N}_{2}$ (i.e. $\left|\Delta E_{\mathrm{N_{2}}}\right|<23$ $\mathrm{\kJ/mol}]$ . They also show weaker affinities for ${\sf N}_{2}$ than the analogous members of the M-MOF-74 series. We note that the substitutions invoked for both the MOF-74/MOF-74-S and MAFOH/MAF-Cl families are with respect to the equatorial ligands. If the modified ligands were in the axial position, the adsorption process can also be influenced by structural trans effects, which may need to be considered.84–87
To further emphasize the degree of control over the $0_{2}$ adsorption energies that is possible via this anion exchange approach, the equatorial bridging anions in MAF-Cl were exchanged with a wider range of species. Varying both the metal and bridging anion, we considered $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{V}^{2+}-\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}$ and bridging ligands of (in order of increasing $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathsf{a}}$ of the parent acid) $^{88,89}\upmu\mathrm{-}$ Br-, $\scriptstyle|\mathsf{u-C l}-$ , $\upmu{-}\upmathrm{F}{-}$ , $\|{\mathsf{I}}{\mathsf{I}}{\mathsf{I}}{\mathsf{I}}-{\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{H}}{\mathsf{I}}{\mathsf{I}} $ , and $\scriptstyle\mathsf{\mu-O H}.$ . As shown in Figure 6, there is a qualitative trend in the $0_{2}$ adsorption energy as a function of the bridging ligand, with stronger $0_{2}$ binding found in MOFs that have more basic ligands. In addition, the ionic radius of the bridging ligand may play a complementary role in this family of MOFs, as smaller bridging anions allow for closer contact between the metals and their coordinating, equatorial donor ligands (Table S4). Based on the results in Figure 6, it is clear that the reactivity of the metal site can be finely tuned via judicious selection of the metal and bridging ligand. Nonetheless, of the Mn-, Co-, and Ni-containing MOFs in Figure 6, only Co-MAF-F, Co-MAF-SH, and Co-MAF-OH are predicted to bind $0_{2}$ more strongly than their M-MOF-74 counterpart – an important comparison given that M-MOF-74 ${\bf\tilde{M}}={\bf M}{\bf n}.$ , Co, Ni) does not chemisorb $0_{2}$ at room temperature.25,90 All members of the Co-MAFX series investigated in this work are predicted to have weak interactions with ${\sf N}_{2}$ (Table S5).
In addition to altering the atomic number of the metal or the basicity of the ligands, one can also consider modifying the oxidation state of the binding site. This is exemplified with the MIL-100 and MIL-101 families, where the presence or lack of charge-balancing OH- anions at the trimetallic nodes can be used to tune the formal oxidation state of the metal (Figure S1, Table S6).91,92 Since the MIL-100 and MIL-101 series have over one thousand atoms per unit cell, we chose to study the MIL88B family, which has the same trimetallic node and terephthalic acid linkers as MIL-101 but is more computationally tractable due to its smaller unit cell size. For this family of MOFs, the formally trivalent transition metals exhibit significantly weaker adsorption of $0_{2}$ than the lower valent analogues (Figure 7), as higher oxidation states reduce the ability of the metal site to transfer electron density to the $0_{2}$ adsorbate. A similar trend is observed for ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption, although both $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ sites of MIL-88B-OH are predicted to adsorb ${\sf N}_{2}$ at least as strong (if not stronger) than their MIL-88B counterparts. In agreement with prior experiments that have shown trimetallic nodes of Cr-MIL-100 and Cr-MIL-101 selectively adsorb ${\sf N}_{2}$ over $0_{2},{}^{93}$ the closely related Cr-MIL-88B-OH with $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ open metal sites is predicted to adsorb ${\sf N}_{2}$ more strongly than $0_{2}$ by approximately $20\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ . Given these results, we would not suggest MOFs with trivalent cations for the selective binding of $0_{2}$ over ${\sf N}_{2},$ although they may be of interest for selective ${\sf N}_{2}$ binding.
## Enhanced, Selective Binding of $O_{2}$ in $C_{02}(O H)_{2}(B B\top A)$
Of the MOFs screened in this work, we chose to investigate CoMAF-OH , as its $0_{2}$ adsorption energy far exceeds that of CoMAF-Cl and all other cobalt-containing MOFs considered in this study (Figure 2a and Figure 6). When combined with the fact that ${\sf N}_{2}$ is predicted to weakly physisorb at the open metal sites (Figure 2b), Co-MAF-OH is predicted to have the largest difference in $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ binding energies of all the Mn-, Fe-, Co-, Ni-, Cu-, and $Z\mathrm{n}$ -containing MOFs studied in this work (Figure S5). Perhaps most importantly, Co-MAF-OH serves as a clear example to highlight the effect of bridging ligand identity on the thermodynamic favorability of $0_{2}$ binding; of all the tested transition metals, it is predicted to have the greatest difference in $0_{2}$ binding energy when the $\mathsf{\Pi}\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C l-}$ ligands of the parent M-MAF-Cl framework are exchanged with $\upmu{\mathrm{-}}0\mathrm{H}^{\cdot}$ ligands (Figure 6). The corresponding non-diluted structure with all cobalt cations at the nodes is known to be stable when exposed to air as well as water, and it is thermally stable up to ${\sim}400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a ${\sf N}_{2}$ environment.43,44 For clarity, we will refer to the non-diluted variants of Co-MAF-OH and Co-MAF-Cl as $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ and $\mathbf{Co}2\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ (BBTA), respectively.
At the PBE-D3(BJ) $+U_{I}$ PAW level of theory – chosen to enable direct comparison with the experimentally determined $0_{2}$ binding enthalpy in Co-BTTri (Table S7) – the enthalpy of $0_{2}$ adsorption in $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ is predicted to be $\Delta H_{0_{2}}=-45$ $\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ in the low-coverage limit compared to $\Delta H_{0_{2}}=-19$ $\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ for $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ at the same level of theory. In contrast, ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption at the open Co(II) sites of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ is predicted to be very weak, with $\Delta H_{\mathrm{N}_{2}}=-14$ $\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ . The predicted $0_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy with $\mathrm{Co2(OH)_{2}(B B T A)}$ is stronger than that of the triazolate-bound Co(II) sites of Co-BTTri $(Q_{s t}=-34~\mathrm{kJ/mol})$ and comparable to that of the pyrazolate-bound Co(II) sites in the mixed triazolate/pyrazolate framework Co-BDTriP $(Q_{s t}=-47~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ at low-loadings), with similarly weak ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption.22
Based on our predictions, we chose to experimentally investigate $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ and $\mathrm{Co2(OH)_{2}(B B T A)}$ to support the hypothesis that exchanging the $\upmu$ -Cl- ligands of $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ with $\scriptstyle\left.\mu-0\mathrm{H}\right.^{\cdot}$ ligands would increase the $0_{2}$ affinity of the Co(II) open metal sites. The PXRD patterns are consistent with those that would be expected for $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathbf{BBTA})$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}$ (BBTA) based on prior reports of their crystal structures (Figure S11).44 The IR spectra, via the presence of an $0{\mathrm{-H}}$ stretch at $3650~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Co2(OH)_{2}(B B T A)}$ but not for $\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}(\mathsf{B B T A})$ , and the XPS spectra, via the presence of a broad 197.8 eV peak in the Cl $2\mathsf{p}$ core level spectrum of $\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}(\mathsf{B B T A})$ but not $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ , both indicate that the chloride anions have been exchanged with hydroxide anions in the $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ material (Figures S13−S16).
The $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ isotherms at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ for $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ are shown in Figure 9. There is a sharp rise in the $0_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at low pressures for $\mathrm{Co2(OH)_{2}(B B T A)}$ , indicative of chemisorption. This is accompanied by a large hysteresis in the $0_{2}$ adsorption and desorption branches, which suggests $0_{2}$ is chemically bound to the open metal sites. In stark contrast, the $298\mathrm{~K~}0_{2}$ isotherm for $\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}(\mathsf{B B T A})$ shows a much shallower rise in the $0_{2}$ uptake at low pressures without any signs of chemisorption. Collectively, these results are consistent with an increased $0_{2}$ affinity enabled by post-synthetically exchanging the $\scriptstyle|\mathsf{u-C l-}$ ligands with $\upmu\mathrm{.}$ 一 $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ ligands, as predicted by DFT. Grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations further corroborate the finding that $\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}(\mathsf{B B T A})$ solely exhibits $0_{2}$ physisorption, whereas $0_{2}$ chemisorption is present for ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}$ (BBTA) (Figure S21).
As expected on the basis of the DFT calculations, the ${\sf N}_{2}$ isotherms at $298\mathrm{K}$ for $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}$ (BBTA) are both consistent with weak physisorption and are similar for both materials (Figure 9). As a result of the large difference in $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ binding in $\mathrm{Co2(OH)_{2}(B B T A)}$ , ideal adsorbed solution theory99,100 predicts an $0_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ selectivity of 49 at $298~\mathrm{K},0.21$ bar $0_{2},$ and 0.79 bar ${\sf N}_{2}$ (Figure S17), which is particularly high compared to that of most other MOFs reported in the literature for air separation applications.18–20,22,23,101–103 For reference, Co-BTTri has a selectivity of 41 at $195\ \mathrm{K},$ but this greatly decreases to only 13 at $243\mathrm{K};$ Co-BDTriP has a selectivity of 40 at $243\mathrm{K},$ with a significantly lower selectivity expected at $298\mathrm{K}^{22}$
The binding energies resulting from the DFT screening procedure describe the thermodynamic favorability of $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption, making it possible to gain fundamental insights and identify promising MOF candidates to investigate in greater detail. While the binding strengths of $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ are perhaps the most important properties to compute, they are not the only factors to consider once MOF promising candidates are identified. In the case of $\mathrm{Co2(OH)_{2}(B B T A)}$ , a significantly longer time is needed for the $0_{2}$ uptake to reach equilibrium than for $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ (BBTA) (Figure S18). $\mathrm{Co2(OH)_{2}(B B T A)}$ is predicted to undergo an $S=5/2$ to $S=1/2$ transition (i.e. $^4\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\ +\ ^{3}\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow$ $^2[\mathsf{C o O}_{2}]^{2+})$ , and large differences in the net spin multiplicity (e.g. $\Delta S=-4\AA$ ) often result in slow kinetics.104 In this scenario, the rate of room temperature $0_{2}$ adsorption could likely be increased by stabilizing low-spin Co(II) sites $(\Delta S=-1)$ or by considering metals that remain high-spin upon $0_{2}$ adsorption, as is predicted for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ $(\Delta S=0)$ ) and $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})(\Delta S=-2)$ (Table S1), both of which have not yet been synthesized.
Overall, the ability of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ to strongly bind $0_{2}$ suggests that this and related MOFs may be of interest for oxidation reactions and other processes involving the presence of redox-active open metal sites, especially when compared to the Cl-containing analogue. Based on the pronounced difference in $0_{2}$ affinities for $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ and $\mathrm{Co2(OH)_{2}(B B T A)}$ , post-synthetic ligand-exchange is likely to serve as a promising and accessible route to control the $0_{2}$ binding affinity in a wide range of MOFs.
## Conclusions
Through the use of periodic density functional theory, we modeled the adsorption of $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ at the open metal sites of several pairs of structurally related MOF families. A comparison of the $\mathsf{M}(\mathrm{II})_{2}(\mathsf{D O B D C})$ and $\mathsf{M}(\mathrm{II})_{2}(\mathsf{D S B D C})$ frameworks suggests that oxido bridges are preferable to sulfido bridges with regards to the adsorption of both $0_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ . Investigation of a topologically similar framework with triazolate (rather than carboxylate) linkers, $\mathrm{M(II)}_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ $[\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , Cl, Br, OH, SH), indicates that the choice of equatorial, bridging anions in this material can be used to greatly alter the $0_{2}$ binding affinity without significantly changing the ${\sf N}_{2}$ binding affinity. We show that there is a qualitative correlation between the $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathsf{a}}$ of the bridging anion and the strength of $0_{2}$ adsorption at the open metal sites, with more basic bridging ligands resulting in more redoxactive metal centers. In general, we find that the strength of $0_{2}$ binding is a strong function of the degree of charge transfer. Consistent with this, early transition metals in low-valence states strongly bind $0_{2}$ due to the presence of readily oxidized metal centers. With regards to ${\sf N}_{2}$ binding, most of the MOFs in this work are not capable of significantly reducing the ${\sf N}_{2}$ molecule, with the primary exception being MOFs that contain synthetically elusive $\mathsf{V}^{2+}$ sites, regardless of the coordination environment of the binding site.
As a result of the quantum-chemical screening process and the trends described in this work, we predicted that exchanging the $\scriptstyle|\mathsf{u-C l^{-}}$ ligands of $\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}(\mathsf{B B T A})$ with more basic $\upmu{\mathrm{-}}0\mathrm{H}^{\cdot}$ groups to form $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ would significantly increase the $0_{2}$ affinity of the Co(II) open metal sites without strengthening their interaction with ${\sf N}_{2}$ . The strong affinity for $0_{2}$ at the Co(II) centers is associated with a significant degree of charge transfer that enables the formation of thermodynamically favored cobalt−superoxo species with the possibility of H-bonding interactions between the $0_{2}$ adsorbate and $\scriptstyle\mathsf{\mu-O H}.$ groups. Experimentally determined adsorption isotherms at room temperature support the DFT predictions that ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}$ (BBTA) chemisorbs $0_{2}$ , whereas $0_{2}$ weakly physisorbs at the open metal sites of $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{BBTA})$ . Weak interactions with ${\sf N}_{2}$ occur in both materials. More generally, we have demonstrated that anion exchange can serve as a simple route to tune the redox activity of coordinatively unsaturated metal sites incorporated within the inorganic nodes of MOFs, enabling the strong and selective room temperature binding of $0_{2}$ in an otherwise unreactive framework.
